@startuml Decorator-Visual-Skin-And-Guts
skinparam linetype polyline
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<spacer>> transparent
  BorderColor<<spacer>> transparent
  FontColor<<spacer>> transparent
}
hide <<spacer>> stereotype
hide <<spacer>> circle
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title GoF implementation issue 4 (skin versus guts) — both patterns, doing different jobs

interface VisualComponent {
  + width() : int
  + height() : int
  + draw(canvas, x, y) : void
}

class TextView {
  + draw(canvas, x, y) : void
}

abstract class Decorator {
  - component : VisualComponent
  # component() : VisualComponent
  + draw(canvas, x, y) : void
}

VisualComponent <|.. TextView
VisualComponent <|.. Decorator

class SwitchingBorderDecorator {
  - style : Style
  + draw(canvas, x, y) : void
}

enum Style {
  SOLID
  DASHED
  THICK
}

class StyledBorderDecorator {
  - style : BorderStyle
  + draw(canvas, x, y) : void
}

interface BorderStyle {
  + stroke(canvas, x, y, w, h) : void
}

class SolidBorder {
  + stroke(...) : void
}

class DashedBorder {
  + stroke(...) : void
}

class ThickBorder {
  + stroke(...) : void
}

Decorator <|-- SwitchingBorderDecorator
Decorator <|-- StyledBorderDecorator
BorderStyle <|.. SolidBorder
BorderStyle <|.. DashedBorder
BorderStyle <|.. ThickBorder

note right of TextView
  **The skin, in both designs.**
  It is wrapped, and it knows nothing
  about borders or styles. A test asserts
  that it names no type from this package.
end note

note bottom of SwitchingBorderDecorator
  The decorator **decides**.
  A fourth style edits draw() and the
  enum — and retests the other three.
end note

note bottom of StyledBorderDecorator
  The decorator **asks**.
  A fourth style is a new class; nothing
  in this one changes. That is the guts
  half, and it is Strategy.
end note

note as N1
  Decorator and Strategy are not alternatives here — they are stacked.
  Decorator adds a responsibility to an object that was never designed
  for it. Strategy varies one decision inside an object that was.

  GoF, p. 180: Strategy is the better answer "where the Component class
  is intrinsically heavyweight", because a decorator pays for the whole
  Component interface and a strategy pays only for the hook.
end note

class " " as Spacer1 <<spacer>>
VisualComponent -[hidden]- Spacer1
VisualComponent -[hidden]- Spacer1
Decorator -[hidden]right- Spacer1
Spacer1 -[hidden]right- TextView
class " " as Spacer2 <<spacer>>
Decorator -[hidden]- Spacer2
Decorator -[hidden]- Spacer2
SwitchingBorderDecorator -[hidden]right- Spacer2
Spacer2 -[hidden]right- StyledBorderDecorator
BorderStyle -[hidden]- DashedBorder
BorderStyle -[hidden]- DashedBorder
SolidBorder -[hidden]right- DashedBorder
DashedBorder -[hidden]right- ThickBorder
Decorator o-[norank]-> "1" VisualComponent : component
SwitchingBorderDecorator -[norank]-> Style : reads
StyledBorderDecorator o-[norank]-> "1" BorderStyle : style
@enduml
